# gsmnode all-in-one — production config, SeaweedFS split into its roles and
# built into the same image (Dockerfile.seaweedfs.split).
# Copy to .env and fill in, then:
#   docker compose -f docker-compose.prod.seaweedfs.split.yml build
#   docker compose -f docker-compose.prod.seaweedfs.split.yml up -d
# The build needs the repo checkout; to deploy elsewhere, set AIO_IMAGE to a
# registry tag, `push` after building and `pull` on the deploy host.

# --- Image -------------------------------------------------------------------
# The tag the build produces and `push`/`pull` use. Its own name, not
# gsmnode-aio: this image carries SeaweedFS and expects four volumes.
AIO_IMAGE=gsmnode-aio-seaweedfs-split
# Build args. SEAWEED_VERSION is pinned in the Dockerfile; set to override.
# PB_VERSION=
# SEAWEED_VERSION=4.45

# --- PocketBase superuser (required) -----------------------------------------
# Created/updated on first boot. The API Server uses these to reach the database.
PB_ADMIN_EMAIL=admin@example.com
PB_ADMIN_PASSWORD=change-me-long-password

# --- gsmnode super-admin (app login) ----------------------------------------
# The first application user, created by the API Server on boot with role
# "superadmin" if no user with this email exists yet. This is the account you log
# in to the web app with — distinct from the PocketBase superuser above. Leave
# blank to skip and create the first user by hand.
GSMNODE_SUPERADMIN_EMAIL=owner@example.com
GSMNODE_SUPERADMIN_PASSWORD=change-me-long-password
GSMNODE_SUPERADMIN_NAME=Administrator
# Set to false to skip schema creation/reconcile once the database is set up.
PB_BOOTSTRAP=true

# --- API Server --------------------------------------------------------------
# How long a message/call may stay unprocessed before it's marked Failed.
MESSAGE_TTL=5m
# Allowed CORS origin(s) — match your public web URL / WEB_PORT.
CORS_ALLOW_ORIGINS=http://localhost:8090

# --- Host port mappings (optional; defaults shown) --------------------------
WEB_PORT=8090
PB_PORT=8070
API_PORT=8080

# --- Storage -----------------------------------------------------------------
# Default is a Docker-managed named volume ("pb_data"). Set PB_DATA to an
# absolute host path for a bind mount, e.g. PB_DATA=/srv/gsmnode/pb_data
PB_DATA=pb_data
# The API Server's own state: plugin enable-state and config (plugins.json) plus
# the settings the panel persists. Separate from the database, and worth backing
# up with it — plugin config holds credentials such as IMAP/SMTP logins.
API_DATA=api_data

# --- File storage: SeaweedFS -------------------------------------------------
# PocketBase keeps its record files — MMS attachments, sent and received — in
# the bucket below instead of on PB_DATA. The database and PocketBase's own
# backups stay where they are.
#
# The credentials do double duty: the S3 gateway's program writes them into the
# filer's IAM store as the identity named "gsmnode" *and* they are what
# PocketBase authenticates with. There are no safe defaults, and the container
# refuses to start without them. Change them here and restart to rotate: the
# seed updates the identity in place rather than adding a second one.
PB_S3_ACCESS_KEY=
PB_S3_SECRET=
# The bucket. Created on first boot by the S3 gateway's program.
PB_S3_BUCKET=gsmnode
# The endpoint, region and path style are fixed by the image — the gateway is
# inside the container at a loopback address that cannot change.

# SEAWEED_DATA — where SeaweedFS keeps the files. A Docker-managed named volume
# by default; set an absolute host path for a bind mount, the same way PB_DATA
# works above. Back it up alongside PB_DATA: from here on the attachments live
# here, not in the database volume. It is mounted at /seaweed/data, the layout
# `weed server -dir` writes — the same one every SeaweedFS shape uses, so they
# are interchangeable on it.
SEAWEED_DATA=seaweed_data
# The S3 gateway publishes no host port: PocketBase reaches it inside the
# container.

# --- SeaweedFS admin UI ------------------------------------------------------
# Cluster topology, volumes, buckets, maintenance tasks, and Object Store →
# Users, where further S3 identities are created and revoked. They land in the
# filer's IAM store, the same one seeded above, and the gateway picks them up
# without a restart.
#
# REQUIRED: weed disables authentication entirely when the password is empty,
# and this panel can mint credentials for the bucket.
SEAWEED_ADMIN_USER=admin
SEAWEED_ADMIN_PASSWORD=
# Optional view-only login.
SEAWEED_ADMIN_READONLY_USER=
SEAWEED_ADMIN_READONLY_PASSWORD=
# Bound to localhost by default. On a remote host that means unreachable — set
# 0.0.0.0 and put it behind a reverse proxy.
SEAWEED_ADMIN_BIND=127.0.0.1
SEAWEED_ADMIN_PORT=23646
# Its own small volume: session key and maintenance-task state, no object data.
SEAWEED_ADMIN_DATA=seaweed_admin

# Existing MMS attachments are NOT migrated when this is switched on:
# PocketBase copies nothing, so attachments stored before the switch stop
# resolving. Read the file storage section of ../Docker/README.md first.
